{"id":"spp-1-underwater-pistol","slug":"spp-1-underwater-pistol","name":"SPP-1","aliases":["SSP-1","SPP-1M","SPP-1 underwater pistol"],"kind":"firearm","category":"firearms","subcategory":"Soviet four-barreled underwater dart pistol","description":"The SPP-1 underwater pistol was made in the Soviet Union for use by Soviet frogmen as an underwater firearm. It was developed in the late 1960s and accepted for use in 1975. Under water, standard bullets are inaccurate and have very short range. This pistol instead fires a round-based 4.5 millimetres (0.18 in) caliber steel dart about 115 millimetres (4.5 in) long, weighing 12.8 grams (0.45 oz), which has longer range and more penetrating power than a speargun. The complete cartridge is 145 millimetres (5.7 in) long and weighs 17.5 grams (0.62 oz).","development":"The SPP-1 has four barrels, each containing one cartridge. Its ammunition comes as a magazine of four cartridges which is inserted into the pistol's breech.\nIts barrel is not rifled; the fired projectile is kept in line by hydrodynamic effects. As a result, it is somewhat inaccurate when fired out of water.\nA double-action firing mechanism fires one cartridge sequentially for each pull of the trigger. When all four cartridges are spent, the gun can be reloaded above or below water.\nThe SPP-1M pistol is essentially the same as the SPP-1, with the following differences:\nIt has an extra spring above the sear to improve the trigger pull.\nIts trigger guard is larger to accommodate diving gloves.\nThe weapon was designed by Vladimir Simonov, the cartridge by Pyotr Sazonov and Oleg Kravchenko. Simonov also designed the APS amphibious rifle.","facts":[],"specifications":[{"group":"Cartridge & Action","items":[{"label":"Cartridge","value":"4.5×40mmR"},{"label":"Action","value":"double action"},{"label":"Muzzle Velocity","value":"240–250 m/s in air"},{"label":"Feed System","value":"4 barrels with a cartridge in each"}]},{"group":"Dimensions","items":[{"label":"Weight","value":"950 g empty,","numeric":950,"unit":"g"},{"label":"Weight","value":"1030 g loaded","numeric":1030,"unit":"g"},{"label":"Length","value":"244 mm","numeric":244,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Width","value":"37 mm","numeric":37,"unit":"mm"},{"label":"Height","value":"136 mm","numeric":136,"unit":"mm"}]},{"group":"Performance","items":[{"label":"Effective Range","value":"in air, 15–20 m;"},{"label":"Effective Range","value":"in water,"},{"label":"Effective Range","value":"17 m at depth of 5 m;","numeric":17,"unit":"m"},{"label":"Effective Range","value":"11 m at depth of 20 m;","numeric":11,"unit":"m"}]},{"group":"Production","items":[{"label":"Designer","value":"Vladimir Simonov at"},{"label":"Designer","value":"TsNIITochMash - ЦНИИТочмаш"},{"label":"Designer","value":"(Central Scientific Institute for Precision Machine Building)"},{"label":"Designed","value":"1960s (Late)"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"TOZ (Tulsky Oruzheiny Zavod)"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"(Тульский Оружейный Завод)"},{"label":"Manufacturer","value":"Tula Arms Plant"},{"label":"Produced","value":"1971–present"}]},{"group":"Service","items":[{"label":"Origin","value":"Soviet Union"},{"label":"In Service","value":"1975–present"}]}],"specIndex":{"Cartridge":"4.5×40mmR","Action":"double action","Muzzle Velocity":"240–250 m/s in air","Feed System":"4 barrels with a cartridge in each","Weight":"950 g empty,","Length":"244 mm","Width":"37 mm","Height":"136 mm","Effective Range":"in air, 15–20 m;","Designer":"Vladimir Simonov at","Designed":"1960s (Late)","Manufacturer":"TOZ (Tulsky Oruzheiny Zavod)","Produced":"1971–present","Origin":"Soviet Union","In Service":"1975–present"},"images":{"hero":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/07/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B2%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B9_%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%A1%D0%9F%D0%9F-1%D0%9C_-_%D0%A6%D0%9D%D0%98%D0%98%D0%A2%D0%9E%D0%A7%D0%9C%D0%90%D0%A8_01.jpg","descriptionUrl":"https://commons.wikimedi